WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.568
-Our first guest tonight
is the Vice President of

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:04.704
the United States.
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:07.140
Vice President Kamala Harris,
everyone.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.841
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:08.841 --> 00:00:18.551 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:28.261 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:30.429
Welcome back.
-It's good to be back.

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:32.431
It's good to be back.
-It's very exciting to have you,

00:00:32.431 --> 00:00:34.300
especially to have you here
as the Vice President.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.869
We've had you on the show
before as a senator.

00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:39.839
And I have noticed
the security protocols

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:42.175
have changed a great deal.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:44.043
I don't know
if we're overprotecting

00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:46.513
our vice presidents or
under-protecting our senators.

00:00:46.513 --> 00:00:48.180
[ Laughter ]

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.849
I can't imagine how much

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.417
that part of it
has changed your life.

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:53.319
How have the security protocols

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:55.555
just infringed on being
a normal human being?

00:00:56.756 --> 00:01:01.160
-Well, okay, one example would
be my husband, Doug and I,

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.062
the first Second Gentleman of
the United States.

00:01:03.062 --> 00:01:04.063
-Very exciting.

00:01:04.063 --> 00:01:05.832
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:08.367
I do want to focus -- I feel
like we're not focusing enough

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:10.336
on what white men have
accomplished with your rise.

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:12.137
[ Laughter ]
-I know.

00:01:12.137 --> 00:01:15.275
It's exactly -- We have jokes
about that too.

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:17.376
But, you know, we love
to take walks,

00:01:17.376 --> 00:01:20.246
and these days the walks
are quite different.

00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:22.081
So, I love "The Godfather."

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:24.417
So, okay, do you know that --
Do you know "The Godfather"?

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:26.452
-Yeah, this is not where I
thought your nice walk --

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:28.387
[ Laughter ]
-No, I'm gonna tell you.

00:01:28.387 --> 00:01:32.158
So, you know that scene
when Michael is in Italy,

00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:33.426
he had to leave.
-Yes.

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:35.328
-Okay, and then he's courting
that woman.

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:37.663
And they decide to take a walk
through the village.

00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:41.033
And so the shot is just the two
of them taking this lovely walk,

00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:44.270
and then it pans out and the
whole village is with them?

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:45.271
-Yes.

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:46.673
-That's what our walks are like.

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:48.407
[ Laughter ]

00:01:48.407 --> 00:01:49.875
-A little bit --
a little bit less romantic.

00:01:49.875 --> 00:01:53.046
-It's a little different.
It's a little different. Yeah.

00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:56.149
-So, we mentioned obviously
already in the show, this sort

00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:58.984
of historic act about marijuana,

00:01:58.984 --> 00:02:01.921
and certainly at
the federal level.

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:04.290
The question, I guess is, you
know, it's actually not that

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.859
many people are affected by it,
but it's such a big step in

00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:09.328
the right direction.
What happens next, other than

00:02:09.328 --> 00:02:12.365
just pressuring governors to do
the same on the state level?

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:15.134
Is there -- Do you see
legislation in the future?

00:02:15.134 --> 00:02:18.872
-Well, you know, we've tried
over the years, but I -- let me

00:02:18.872 --> 00:02:20.539
just start with saying this.

00:02:20.539 --> 00:02:24.510
I strongly believe, and
the majority of Americans agree,

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:28.114
nobody should have to go to jail
for smoking weed, right?

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:31.083
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:34.353
Right? And so we start with
that, and then we are,

00:02:34.353 --> 00:02:36.856
to your point, urging -- and the
President has been very clear --

00:02:36.856 --> 00:02:39.925
we're urging governors
and states to take our lead

00:02:39.925 --> 00:02:43.162
and to pardon people who have
been criminalized

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:44.830
for possession of marijuana.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:48.801
And ultimately, though, as with
so many issues, if Congress

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:54.507
acts, then there is a uniform
approach to this

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:56.609
and so many other issues,
but Congress needs to act.

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:59.545
We're 29 days away
from the midterms.

00:02:59.545 --> 00:03:02.147
Ask who you're voting for
where they stand on this,

00:03:02.147 --> 00:03:05.151
and I encourage you
to vote accordingly.

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:07.987
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:11.891
-As an administration, you have
accomplished a great deal

00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:14.193
despite only having
a 50/50 Senate.

00:03:14.193 --> 00:03:15.561
-Yeah.

00:03:15.561 --> 00:03:18.664
-You know, you mentioned 29 days
out from the midterms.

00:03:18.664 --> 00:03:22.768
What is different if you win
two more Senate seats?

00:03:22.768 --> 00:03:25.437
What is the message as far as
what will change for Americans'

00:03:25.437 --> 00:03:28.507
lives if you have that sort of
majority in the Senate

00:03:28.507 --> 00:03:31.177
that isn't weighing on
maybe two Democratic senators

00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:34.113
who aren't always as in-line
with the party?

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:35.815
-There's so much, Seth,
that changes.

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:38.484
So, 29 days.

00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:39.919
The Dobbs decision.

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:43.556
So, the United States
Supreme Court, the highest court

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:48.027
in our land, just took
a constitutional right that had

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:51.297
been recognized
from the people of America,

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:53.766
from the women of America,
right?

00:03:53.766 --> 00:04:00.039
And the significance of that is
profound in terms of what it

00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:03.442
means for the rights of
individuals and, in particular,

00:04:03.442 --> 00:04:06.045
for the right of an individual
to make decisions about

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:07.946
their own body and not have
their government

00:04:07.946 --> 00:04:09.482
tell them what to do.

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:13.218
But in addition, what it means
for the next 29 days is,

00:04:13.218 --> 00:04:16.288
if we keep our numbers in
the Senate and elect two more

00:04:16.288 --> 00:04:20.125
United States Senators, then the
President can sign into law

00:04:20.125 --> 00:04:22.128
the Women's Health Protection
Act, which would put

00:04:22.128 --> 00:04:24.797
into law the protections
of Roe v. Wade.

00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:26.532
So critically important.

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:28.367
29 days to go.

00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:31.671
And the reality of it is that,
on the one hand, 29 days.

00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:35.008
Everyone has a lot going on,
very busy, and it's gonna take

00:04:35.008 --> 00:04:36.709
an effort to make sure
that you vote.

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:40.513
On the other hand, these House
seats, they're two-year terms.

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:42.782
These Senate seats,
six-year terms.

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:44.216
So think about it.

00:04:44.216 --> 00:04:46.819
Whether we let it go with
status quo the way it is now

00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:50.590
for the next two years or six
years, or in the next 29 days.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:54.761
Make a plan to vote and actually
vote on this issue.

00:04:54.761 --> 00:04:55.962
-I think that --

00:04:55.962 --> 00:05:01.166
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:01.166 --> 00:05:05.538
I think correctly, you know,
a lot of people think about

00:05:05.538 --> 00:05:08.140
abortion as an important issue,
but you've also talked about

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:11.343
maternal health, which seems
like that would be the one that

00:05:11.343 --> 00:05:14.880
would seem like everyone could
agree on because the health of

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.018
a mother, the health of a child,
this -- based on what both sides

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:21.553
of the aisle say, this is
deeply important to them.

00:05:21.553 --> 00:05:25.391
And yet it seems as though

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:27.093
we can't even reach a consensus
on that.

00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:28.827
Can you just talk about the
importance of maternal health

00:05:28.827 --> 00:05:31.597
and steps that we can take
to make life better

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:33.566
for certainly young mothers?

00:05:33.566 --> 00:05:35.500
-Yeah, it's a critically
important issue.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.003
And I know you've spoken up
on this over the years.

00:05:38.003 --> 00:05:40.740
In our country today,

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.643
women are still dying in
connection with childbirth.

00:05:43.643 --> 00:05:47.513
And when you look at, then, the
racial disparities, Black women

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:50.650
are three times more likely to
die, Native American women twice

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.985
as likely to die,
women in rural America

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:55.254
1.5 times more likely to die.

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:58.257
And a lot of it has to do with,
again, the disparities

00:05:58.257 --> 00:06:01.193
in the healthcare system, and
that we are not putting enough

00:06:01.193 --> 00:06:06.666
attention and resources
and priority into women's needs,

00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:09.268
especially as it relates to
their reproductive health.

00:06:09.268 --> 00:06:11.370
And I do want to say this also.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.706
This is an issue
that affects all genders.

00:06:15.607 --> 00:06:19.178
If you have a partner, if you
have a sister, if you have

00:06:19.178 --> 00:06:22.381
an aunt, a mother,
this affects you.

00:06:22.381 --> 00:06:25.017
Not to mention on
the Dobbs decision when

00:06:25.017 --> 00:06:28.187
Justice Clarence Thomas said
the quiet part out loud,

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:31.289
which is that when they took the
constitutional right from women

00:06:31.289 --> 00:06:36.128
on reproductive choice,
that he said, that in-line,

00:06:36.128 --> 00:06:38.197
in terms of what
might come next,

00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:40.266
is your right to contraception.

00:06:40.266 --> 00:06:43.068
That will affect all genders.

00:06:43.068 --> 00:06:46.806
Your right to marry the person
you love, same point.

00:06:46.806 --> 00:06:51.210
And so there is so much
on the line with these issues

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:54.346
that affects everyone
and the people you love.

00:06:54.346 --> 00:06:57.683
And for that reason, let's take
it seriously and let's,

00:06:57.683 --> 00:07:02.388
you know, speak with our vote
and undo what I think is a great

00:07:02.388 --> 00:07:05.057
travesty of justice as it
relates to that Dobbs decision.

00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:07.092
-I have a lot more to ask you.
-Okay.

00:07:07.092 --> 00:07:10.562
-I do hope you'll stick around.
We'll be right back with more

00:07:10.562 --> 00:07:12.365
from the Vice President,
everybody.

